According to its graph, the one-sided limits at $x=-3$ are different for function $g$ and therefore $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}g(x)$ doesn't exist.

However, before we jump to any conclusions regarding $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}\Big(g(x)f(x)\Big)$, let's consider the one-sided limits:

$\begin{align}
&\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3^-}g(x)=1}
\\\\
&\purpleD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3^+}g(x)=-1}
\\\\
&\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3^-}f(x)=\lim_{x\to -3^+}f(x)=0}
\end{align}$

We can find each one-sided limit of $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}\Big(g(x)f(x)\Big)$ using the fact that the limit of a product of functions is the product of the individual limits:

$\begin{align}
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3^-}\Big(g(x)f(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to -3^-}g(x)\cdot \lim_{x\to -3^-}f(x)
\\\\
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3^+}\Big(g(x)f(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to -3^+}g(x)\cdot \lim_{x\to -3^+}f(x)
\end{align}$
